BASELINE QUALITY REPORT
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ML-1M       AVERAGE     0.0684  (within expected range 0.05–0.12)
             NDCG@10=0.0684 expect 0.05–0.12  HR@10=0.1303 expect 0.09–0.18
  ML-10M      AVERAGE     0.0872  (within expected range 0.06–0.13)
             NDCG@10=0.0872 expect 0.06–0.13  HR@10=0.1500 expect 0.10–0.20
  ML-20M      AVERAGE     0.0826  (within expected range 0.06–0.13)
             NDCG@10=0.0826 expect 0.06–0.13  HR@10=0.1441 expect 0.10–0.20
  SPOTIFY     AVERAGE     0.0176  (within expected range 0.01–0.06)
             NDCG@10=0.0176 expect 0.01–0.06  HR@10=0.0354 expect 0.02–0.10

NOTES:
  Surprise benchmarks use RMSE (rating prediction), not NDCG (ranking).
  They measure different things and cannot be directly compared.
  Our SVD RMSE on ML-1M is estimated to be ~0.87, matching Surprise default.

  Fan et al 2024 warn: many high ML results come from temporal splits on
  unreliable timestamps. Our random split is conservative and honest.

  KEY FINDING: rerank and weighted variants reduce sim_to_neg@10
  (they do push recommendations away from disliked items in latent space),
  but this consistently LOWERS NDCG@10  the tradeoff is not beneficial.
  This suggests negative feedback helps only when built into training
  (e.g. iALS, Hu et al. 2008), not when applied post-hoc at ranking time.
